TITLE:: PT
summary:: The Pattern Toolbox registry and evaluation environment
categories:: Composition>Pattern Toolbox
related:: Classes/PTObject, Classes/PTStockpile, Classes/PTDataSection

DESCRIPTION::
Every Pattern Toolbox object has a name and lives in one flat registry held by
PT. Object specifications are text, and PT compiles that text inside
link::#*envir::, where every registered object is reachable as code::~name::.

code::
PTStockpile.specify(\cmajor, "c4 d4 e4 f4 g4 a4 b4");

PTDataSection(\section1, (
	clock:  "150",
	number: "50",
	rhythm: "Prand([1, 2, 3], inf)",
	pitch:  "Prand(cmajor, inf)"
)).make.play;

PT.post;
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Look up a registered object by name. code::PT(\section1):: is the usual form.
ARGUMENT:: name
a Symbol.

METHOD:: names
The names of all registered objects, in creation order.
ARGUMENT:: type
optional. A type Symbol such as code::\section:: or code::\stockpile::.

METHOD:: all
The registered objects themselves.

METHOD:: remove
METHOD:: removeAll
Forget one object, or the whole environment.

METHOD:: save
METHOD:: load
Write or read a code::.ptx:: archive. An archive is plain sclang source, one
code::PT.def:: per object, and it carries each object's random seed, so loading
reproduces the exact realization rather than merely the rules.

METHOD:: eval
Compile and run a specification string in the toolbox environment, after name
resolution.

METHOD:: resolve
The source rewrite applied before compiling. A registered object name becomes
code::~name::, a pitch name becomes a note number, a dynamic of two characters or
more becomes a velocity. String literals, symbol literals, method calls and
keyword arguments are left alone.
code::
PT.resolve("Pwhite(c2, c5)");   // -> "Pwhite(36, 72)"
::

METHOD:: sugar
Whether link::#*resolve:: rewrites anything. Default code::true::.

METHOD:: middleCOctave
The octave number of middle C. Default code::4::, as in the AC Toolbox.

METHOD:: note
METHOD:: midinote
Convert a pitch name, or an array of them, to midi note numbers. Numbers pass
through.
code::
PT.note(\ef4);      // 63
PT.note('c#4');     // 61
::

METHOD:: dyn
METHOD:: velocity
Convert a dynamic symbol to a velocity. code::\ppp:: to code::\fff::,
code::\mf:: is 64.

METHOD:: untilTime
For the code::number:: slot of a section: keep making notes until this many
seconds have been filled.

METHOD:: bpm
METHOD:: mm
Beats per minute, or a metronome mark, as a clock unit in milliseconds.

METHOD:: clock
The TempoClock at one beat per second that playback uses. Event durations are in
seconds.

METHOD:: post
Post every registered object with its type, length and seed.
